course: medicine
lesson: 17
title: How medicines work
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A tablet doesn't know where your headache is: it is swallowed, soaks through the gut into the blood, and travels all round the body, acting only on the parts it fits, like a key in a lock.
explain: Calpol is children's paracetamol: it blocks pain signals and lowers fever, but it doesn't cure the cause; your body does the healing.
explain: Other forms put medicine where it's needed: cream on the skin, drops in the eye, an inhaler into the lungs.
explain: New medicines are tested in labs, then in careful trials with volunteers, and checked by regulators before doctors can prescribe them; even natural doesn't always mean safe.
explain: Medicines look like sweets to small children, so they are kept locked up high, out of reach and sight.
fact: Calpol is a brand of paracetamol for children; painkillers block pain signals and lower fever but don't cure the cause.
fact: Most swallowed medicines are absorbed through the gut into the blood and carried all round the body, acting where they find the right targets.
fact: Creams work where they are put, so less medicine goes round the whole body; a thin layer as directed is enough.
fact: Liquid medicine is measured with a measuring syringe or spoon, because kitchen spoons are different sizes.
fact: Eye drops are put in by gently pulling down the lower lid, without touching the eye with the bottle; rubbing the eye straight afterwards can push the medicine out.
fact: Some medicines are spaced through the day to keep a steady level in the blood; taking all the doses at once is dangerous.
fact: Liquid medicine is easier for children to swallow, and the dose can be measured exactly.
fact: Many medicines are naturally bitter, so makers add sweet flavours for children.
fact: A placebo is a dummy treatment with no active medicine; people sometimes feel better just because they expect to.
fact: Scientists compare new medicines with placebos in trials to see if they really work.
fact: Regulators, official checkers, test a medicine before doctors can prescribe (order) it, and side effects (unwanted effects) are watched for after it goes on sale, because rare ones only show up when many people use it.
fact: Natural doesn't always mean safe: some plants are poisonous.
fact: Medicines are kept out of reach and sight of small children; child-resistant lids are not child-proof.
exercise: What does Calpol do for a headache? | Blocks pain signals while the body heals | Cures whatever caused the headache in the first place | Kills all the germs in your head | Makes the tired brain grow back stronger
exercise: How does a swallowed tablet reach your head? | Through the gut into the blood, all round the body | It knows the way to the pain by itself | It climbs back up your throat and into your head to the pain | It stays in the tummy and sends a message
exercise: Why use a cream for a rash instead of a tablet? | It works where it's put, with less medicine round the body | Creams are always much stronger than any tablet | Tablets can't help skin at all | Creams taste better
exercise: Is putting on loads of cream better? | No, a thin layer as directed is enough | Yes, the thicker you put it on, the better it works | Yes, a whole tube at once | No, never use cream on skin
exercise: Why do pharmacists give a measuring syringe for liquid medicine? | Kitchen spoons are different sizes | Syringes are more fun for children to use | Metal spoons poison the medicine | Liquids slide off spoons
exercise: How are eye drops put in? | Pull down the lower lid and drop in without touching the eye | Pour the bottle over the whole face with the eyes shut | Rub the drops in with a clean finger | Put them on the eyelashes
exercise: Why take a medicine three times a day instead of all at once? | To keep a steady level in the blood | Because it tastes better in small amounts | So you remember what time it is | Because the box is too small for a big dose
exercise: Why do many medicines taste horrible? | Many are naturally bitter | Makers add nasty flavours on purpose | So children won't like being ill | Because they are very old by the time you get them
exercise: What is a placebo? | A dummy treatment with no active medicine | A very strong medicine kept for really bad pain | A kind of vitamin for children | A doctor's tool for looking in ears
exercise: Why do scientists use placebos in trials? | To see if a new medicine works better than nothing | To trick the patients for fun | To save money on real medicine | Because the real medicines have run out in the hospital
exercise: How do they know a medicine is safe? | Lab tests, trials with volunteers and checks by regulators | Someone at the factory tastes it once | It is safe if it is natural | It looks safe in the packet
exercise: Why are medicines kept locked up high? | Small children might think they are sweets | They need to stay cold up near the ceiling | They go off if they're left down on the floor | Dogs and cats like chewing the cardboard boxes
exercise: Are child-resistant lids child-proof? | No, some children can still open them | Yes, no child can ever open them, whatever they try | Yes, if they are screwed on tight | No, they are made for babies to open
exercise: Does natural always mean safe? | No, some plants are poisonous | Yes, always, if it grew in the ground | Yes, as long as it's green and leafy | No, only the herbs from the shop are safe
exercise: Why might a child get liquid medicine instead of tablets? | It is easier to swallow and to dose exactly | Liquid medicine is always stronger than tablets | Tablets are for adults only by law | Liquid medicine has no taste at all
test: Calpol fixed my headache, so it cured whatever caused it, right? | No, it eases the pain while the body heals | Yes, painkillers always cure the cause | Yes, and it stops all future headaches too | No, it makes headaches worse later
test: My brother asks how the tablet knows to go to his head. What do you tell him? | It goes everywhere in the blood and acts where it fits | It has a tiny map inside it | The brain calls it up when it hurts | It doesn't, it stays in the tummy and works from there
test: Mum used a kitchen teaspoon for my medicine. What's the problem? | Kitchen spoons are different sizes, so the dose may be wrong | Metal spoons ruin the medicine | Teaspoons are always too small for any dose | No problem at all
test: Forgot two doses, so I'll take three at once to catch up. OK? | No, taking doses all at once is dangerous | Yes, that's how you catch up | Yes, double or triple is always fine if you missed some | No, take five instead to be safe
test: My friend says the herbal pills are natural so they can't hurt you. What's wrong? | Natural doesn't always mean safe | Nothing, he's right about herbal pills | Herbs can't have side effects | Natural pills are tested more than others
test: Aunt felt better on a sugar pill in a trial. Was she faking? | No, people can really feel better because they expect to | Yes, she was lying to the doctors | Yes, sugar is a real medicine | No, sugar cures everything if you believe
test: In a cartoon, a baby climbs the cupboard and eats pills thinking they're sweets. What's the lesson? | Keep medicines locked up out of reach and sight | Babies can't climb | Pills are just sweets | Keep only the sweets up high, pills can stay low
test: What would happen if new medicines were sold without any trials? | Nobody would know if they worked or were safe | They would work better | They would be cheaper and much safer for everyone | Nothing would change at all
test: Ugh, rubbed my eye straight after the drops. Problem? | Rubbing can push the medicine out | It helps the drops work much quicker | It makes the drops stronger and last longer | No, rub hard to spread the drops round
test: Why do doctors keep watching a medicine even after it's on sale? | Rare side effects can show up in many users | To count the money | To change its colour | They don't, they stop watching once it's in the shops
test: Why did the doctor give you cream instead of a tablet for your rash? | Cream works on the skin where it's needed | Tablets can't reach skin at all | Cream is always stronger than any tablet you can swallow | Tablets are only for adults
exam: Identify the means by which an orally administered medicine reaches the site of pain. | It is absorbed into the blood and distributed throughout the body | It is directed by the nervous system straight to the painful site | It remains within the stomach | It travels through the throat directly to the head
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a placebo? | A preparation containing no active medicine, employed in trials for comparison | A medicine of exceptional potency | A vitamin supplement | An instrument used in examination
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the approval of new medicines. | They are tested in laboratories and in trials with volunteers and reviewed by regulators | They are approved upon a single tasting | They are deemed safe if derived from plants | They are approved without any assessment
exam: State the reason for which liquid medicines are measured with a calibrated syringe. | Household spoons vary in capacity | Syringes alter the strength of the medicine | Spoons react chemically with medicine | Syringes are required by school rules
